<main id="main">
<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/documents.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/documents-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/documents.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="601" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="shell sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span><span aria-current="page">How appraisal works</span></nav><p class="kicker">Process guide</p><h1>How Insurance Appraisal Works in Utah, Step by Step</h1><p class="lede">Eight steps from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what, and where claims most often go wrong.</p><div class="row-btns"><a class="btn btn-accent" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/contact-utah-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Steps</dt><dd>8</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="shell two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="Steps"><b>On this page</b><ol><li><a href="#step-1">Confirm it’s an amount dispute</a></li><li><a href="#step-2">Document and supplement</a></li><li><a href="#step-3">Make the written demand</a></li><li><a href="#step-4">Each side names an appraiser</a></li><li><a href="#step-5">The appraisers pick an umpire</a></li><li><a href="#step-6">Appraisers work the differences</a></li><li><a href="#step-7">Umpire decides what’s left</a></li><li><a href="#step-8">The award is signed and paid</a></li></ol></nav>
<p>Insurance appraisal is a contract process written into most Utah property policies. It isn’t a lawsuit and it isn’t mediation. It’s a way to have the amount of a covered loss decided by a small panel when you and your insurer can’t agree. The steps below describe how it usually unfolds. Your policy’s wording controls, so read your own appraisal condition alongside this guide.</p>
<h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Confirm it’s an amount dispute</h2><p>Appraisal only answers one question: how much the covered damage costs. Confirm the carrier has accepted coverage for at least part of the loss and that the disagreement is about scope or price.</p><p>Signs that appraisal is the wrong tool include an outright denial, a dispute over whether damage was caused by a covered peril, or an argument about an exclusion such as wear and tear. Those are coverage questions, which generally belong with an attorney.</p><h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Document and supplement</h2><p>Gather the carrier’s full estimate, your own estimate, photos and correspondence. Submit a written supplement request explaining what’s missing. Many disputes close here.</p><ul><li>The carrier’s complete line-item estimate, not just the summary page</li><li>Your contractor’s or independent estimate, also itemized</li><li>Dated photos, measurements and samples of damaged materials</li><li>All letters and emails in date order</li></ul><h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Make the written demand</h2><p>If the gap remains, either party can demand appraisal in writing under the policy’s appraisal condition, naming or promising to name an appraiser.</p><p>Send the demand in a way you can prove, such as certified mail plus email. Keep a copy. Check whether your policy requires anything first, such as a sworn proof of loss.</p><h2 id="step-4">Step 4: Each side names an appraiser</h2><p>You and the insurer each select a competent, impartial appraiser, usually within a set number of days stated in the policy.</p><p>Choose someone independent of the repair. An appraiser with a financial stake in the contract can give the other side grounds to challenge the award. Get the fee arrangement in writing.</p><h2 id="step-5">Step 5: The appraisers pick an umpire</h2><p>The two appraisers agree on a neutral umpire. If they can’t, many policies allow a court in the county where the property sits to appoint one.</p><p>A good umpire is experienced with the type of loss, has no conflicts and is willing to disclose prior work with either appraiser.</p><h2 id="step-6">Step 6: Appraisers work the differences</h2><p>The appraisers inspect, compare measurements and pricing, and agree on as many items as they can. Many files end with an agreed award.</p><p>This stage is where documentation pays off. Clear measurements, photos and pricing support make it easier for the other appraiser to agree.</p><h2 id="step-7">Step 7: Umpire decides what’s left</h2><p>Remaining items go to the umpire, who reviews both positions and the evidence, then joins one appraiser on each disputed item.</p><p>The umpire may inspect the property, ask for written positions or hold an informal meeting. The umpire usually decides only the items still in dispute.</p><h2 id="step-8">Step 8: The award is signed and paid</h2><p>Two signatures set the amount of loss. The insurer then pays according to the policy, less deductibles and prior payments.</p><p>A clear award separates replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value. Recoverable depreciation is typically paid once repairs are completed within the policy’s time limit.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Fees</b>Each side pays its own appraiser. Umpire fees are commonly split equally. Ask for fee terms in writing before you sign anything.</div>
<h2>What appraisal doesn’t decide</h2>
<ul>
<li>Whether a loss is covered or excluded.</li>
<li>Whether the insurer handled the claim properly or owes anything beyond the policy.</li>
<li>Questions of law, such as how a policy term should be interpreted.</li>
</ul>
<p>For those questions, talk with a Utah attorney. The Utah Insurance Department also accepts consumer complaints about how claims are handled.</p>
<h2>Utah timing to keep in mind</h2>
<p>Utah law generally gives policyholders three years from the inception of a loss to bring an action on a first-party property policy (Utah Code § 31A-21-313). Policies can contain their own deadlines for notice, proof of loss and recovering depreciation. Appraisal doesn’t automatically pause those clocks, so track them separately. Our <a href="/utah-claim-calculators/claim-timeline-planner/">claim timeline planner</a> can help.</p>
